          "title": "Exhaustiveness default returns the event object, not a string",
          "body": "The `default` branch assigns `event` to a `never`-typed local and then returns it. At compile time this is the standard exhaustiveness guard, but the emitted JavaScript is `return event;` \u2014 so if an `Event` with an unrecognised `kind` reaches `describe` at runtime (deserialised JSON, a value from an older client, a new variant added later without recompiling all producers), the function returns an object from a `string`-typed signature. The previous code degraded to the string `\"key undefined\"` in that case, so callers that concatenate or call `.toUpperCase()` on the result now get an object or a TypeError instead. Prefer `throw new Error(\\`unhandled event kind: ${JSON.stringify(event)}\\`)` after the `never` assignment, which keeps the compile-time check and makes the runtime path explicit.",
