              "title": "Exhaustiveness default returns the event object, violating the string return type",
              "body": "`describe` is declared to return `string`, but the `default` branch returns `unreachable`, which is the event value itself at runtime. The `never` type makes the compiler accept this, yet nothing enforces it at runtime: an `Event` deserialised from JSON, a network payload, or a newer producer emitting a third `kind` would flow into `default` and the function would hand a plain object back to a caller that will do string things with it (template interpolation, `.toUpperCase()`, `.length`). The old code degraded to the string `\"key undefined\"` in that situation, so this is also a behaviour change on the unexpected-input path. Prefer `throw new Error(\\`unhandled event kind: ${(event as {kind: string}).kind}\\`)` in the default branch, or return a literal fallback string, keeping the `const unreachable: never = event;` line purely as the compile-time exhaustiveness check.",
